  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The induced current in the wire&nbsp; will be due to the motional EMF developed in the moving resistor&nbsp; and that back emf developed&nbsp; at that point will be&nbsp; equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo></math>velocity of the conductor</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo></math>perpendicular magnetic field</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo></math>length of conductor</p><p>We know magnetic field due to a long wire is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence the induced current&nbsp;across wire will be :</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>Iv</mi><mi>&#8467;</mi></mrow><mi>Rr</mi></mfrac></math></p>",
